@anon.b08dd5f4f09c4be6245191ddd275a566.464 = private unnamed_addr constant [679 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of SNMPv1 or SNMPv2.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe SNMPv1 and SNMPv2 protocols are considered insecure as they do\0Anot support encryption. Instead, prefer SNMPv3, which supports\0Aencryption.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom pysnmp.hlapi import CommunityData\0A\0ACommunityData(\22public\22, mpModel=0)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom pysnmp.hlapi import CommunityData\0A\0ACommunityData(\22public\22, mpModel=2)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA): Alert TA17-156A](https://www.cisa.gov/news-events/alerts/2017/06/05/reducing-risk-snmp-abuse)\0A- [Common Weakness Enumeration: CWE-319](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)\0A", align 1
