@anon.b08dd5f4f09c4be6245191ddd275a566.605 = private unnamed_addr constant [1559 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `is` and `is not` comparisons against literals, like integers,\0Astrings, or lists.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe `is` and `is not` comparators operate on identity, in that they check\0Awhether two objects are the same object. If the objects are not the same\0Aobject, the comparison will always be `False`. Using `is` and `is not` with\0Aconstant literals often works \22by accident\22, but are not guaranteed to produce\0Athe expected result.\0A\0AAs of Python 3.8, using `is` and `is not` with constant literals will produce\0Aa `SyntaxWarning`.\0A\0AThis rule will also flag `is` and `is not` comparisons against non-constant\0Aliterals, like lists, sets, and dictionaries. While such comparisons will\0Anot raise a `SyntaxWarning`, they are still likely to be incorrect, as they\0Awill compare the identities of the objects instead of their values, which\0Awill always evaluate to `False`.\0A\0AInstead, use `==` and `!=` to compare literals, which will compare the\0Avalues of the objects instead of their identities.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Ax = 200\0Aif x is 200:\0A    print(\22It's 200!\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Ax = 200\0Aif x == 200:\0A    print(\22It's 200!\22)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: Identity comparisons](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/expressions.html#is-not)\0A- [Python documentation: Value comparisons](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/expressions.html#value-comparisons)\0A- [_Why does Python log a SyntaxWarning for \E2\80\98is\E2\80\99 with literals?_ by Adam Johnson](https://adamj.eu/tech/2020/01/21/why-does-python-3-8-syntaxwarning-for-is-literal/)\0A", align 1
@anon.b08dd5f4f09c4be6245191ddd275a566.606 = private unnamed_addr constant [37 x i8] c"Use `==` to compare constant literals", align 1
@anon.b08dd5f4f09c4be6245191ddd275a566.607 = private unnamed_addr constant [37 x i8] c"Use `!=` to compare constant literals", align 1
@anon.b08dd5f4f09c4be6245191ddd275a566.608 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ ptr, [8 x i8], ptr, [8 x i8] }> <{ ptr @anon.b08dd5f4f09c4be6245191ddd275a566.606, [8 x i8] c"%\00\00\00\00\00\00\00", ptr @anon.b08dd5f4f09c4be6245191ddd275a566.607, [8 x i8] c"%\00\00\00\00\00\00\00" }>, align 8
