@anon.b08dd5f4f09c4be6245191ddd275a566.628 = private unnamed_addr constant [936 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for functions or methods with too many statements.\0A\0ABy default, this rule allows up to 50 statements, as configured by the\0A[`lint.pylint.max-statements`] option.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AFunctions or methods with many statements are harder to understand\0Aand maintain.\0A\0AInstead, consider refactoring the function or method into smaller\0Afunctions or methods, or identifying generalizable patterns and\0Areplacing them with generic logic or abstractions.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Adef is_even(number: int) -> bool:\0A    if number == 0:\0A        return True\0A    elif number == 1:\0A        return False\0A    elif number == 2:\0A        return True\0A    elif number == 3:\0A        return False\0A    elif number == 4:\0A        return True\0A    elif number == 5:\0A        return False\0A    else:\0A        ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Adef is_even(number: int) -> bool:\0A    return number % 2 == 0\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `lint.pylint.max-statements`\0A", align 1
