@anon.b08dd5f4f09c4be6245191ddd275a566.646 = private unnamed_addr constant [1425 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for subclasses of `dict`, `list` or `str`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ABuilt-in types don't consistently use their own dunder methods. For example,\0A`dict.__init__` and `dict.update()` bypass `__setitem__`, making inheritance unreliable.\0A\0AUse the `UserDict`, `UserList`, and `UserString` objects from the `collections` module\0Ainstead.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Aclass UppercaseDict(dict):\0A    def __setitem__(self, key, value):\0A        super().__setitem__(key.upper(), value)\0A\0A\0Ad = UppercaseDict({\22a\22: 1, \22b\22: 2})  # Bypasses __setitem__\0Aprint(d)  # {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Afrom collections import UserDict\0A\0A\0Aclass UppercaseDict(UserDict):\0A    def __setitem__(self, key, value):\0A        super().__setitem__(key.upper(), value)\0A\0A\0Ad = UppercaseDict({\22a\22: 1, \22b\22: 2})  # Uses __setitem__\0Aprint(d)  # {'A': 1, 'B': 2}\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis fix is marked as unsafe because `isinstance()` checks for `dict`,\0A`list`, and `str` types will fail when using the corresponding User class.\0AIf you need to pass custom `dict` or `list` objects to code you don't\0Acontrol, ignore this check. If you do control the code, consider using\0Athe following type checks instead:\0A\0A* `dict` -> `collections.abc.MutableMapping`\0A* `list` -> `collections.abc.MutableSequence`\0A* `str` -> No such conversion exists\0A\0A## References\0A\0A- [Python documentation: `collections`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/collections.html)\0A", align 1
